{
	title: 'Local notices: toasts for other tabs, push only when nobody watches',
	spec: "A client tells the host which tab it shows while watched: a browser tab while visible and focused; a terminal while its window has focus (it enables terminal focus reporting, CSI ?1004h); a terminal that never reports focus counts as watching whenever it shows a tab. For each event that pushes (turn completed, turn failed, question waiting): if any client watches that session nothing is sent, since the user already sees it; else if any client watches another tab, each such client gets a notice and no push is sent; else a web push as task m1. A notice shows tab number, name, what happened (done, failed, needs your attention) and one line (the reply's last line, the error, or the question). Clients show notices in a stack at the bottom right, just above the tabs: a bar in the event's colour (done green, failed red, attention amber), newest at the bottom, each gone after 5 s, beyond three the oldest fold into '+N more (tab numbers)'; full width on narrow terminals; on the web square cards, a tap opens the tab. The tab's attention mark stays until the tab is visited. No bell (a plugin can add one from the same event). The stack takes entries from any source, and an entry may stay until its source removes it, so later sources (changed files) fit.",
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['m1']
}
